    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Prep Decides Everything</a>
          <a href="#assess" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Assessing Your Slab</a>
          <a href="#moisture" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Moisture Testing</a>
          <a href="#grind-vs-etch" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Grinding vs Etching</a>
          <a href="#grinding" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Grind a Floor</a>
          <a href="#repairs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cracks, Pits and Oil</a>
          <a href="#final-check" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Final Checklist</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="19.0k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Epoxy does not glue itself to concrete. It flows into the open pores and microscopic texture of the surface and locks in mechanically as it cures. That means the coating can only ever be as good as the surface it keyed into — and a slab with a curing compound, a layer of laitance, or a film of old sealer gives it nothing to key into at all. Prep is not the boring part before the real job. Prep is the job.
        </p>

        <div id="why-prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Prep Decides Everything</h2>
          <p>
            When a garage floor coating peels, homeowners usually blame the product. In the overwhelming majority of cases the product was fine and the bond was never there. There are three specific culprits, and all of them are invisible to the eye.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first is <strong>laitance</strong> — the weak, fine layer of cement paste and water that rises to the surface as concrete is finished. It looks like concrete, feels like concrete, and has a fraction of the strength. Coat over it and the epoxy bonds perfectly to a layer that is itself barely attached to the slab. The coating comes off later carrying a thin skin of concrete on its back, which is the telltale sign.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is <strong>curing compound</strong>. Most new slabs get sprayed with a membrane-forming compound to hold moisture in during the first week. It is doing an important job and it is fatal to adhesion, and it is not visible once dry. Any new-construction garage should be assumed to have it until proven otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            The third is <strong>an existing sealer</strong>. Builders often apply an acrylic sealer to garage slabs. Some previous owner may have applied one too. A sealer sheds water, which is exactly why a coating cannot penetrate it either.
          </p>
          <p>
            Grinding removes all three in one pass. That is the whole argument for it, and it is why every manufacturer's warranty language quietly hinges on surface preparation.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum cure on new concrete</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">CSP 2-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Target profile for roll-on epoxy</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3 lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical max vapor emission per 1,000 sq ft</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">60-70%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Share of the job that is prep time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Assessing Your Slab First</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Water Drop Test</h3>
          <p>
            Sprinkle water in six or eight places across the floor, including inside the door, in the wheel paths and along the back wall. Concrete that darkens and absorbs within a minute is open and porous. Water that beads or sits in a puddle means something is sealing that spot. Test multiple areas, because slabs are rarely uniform — the section under where a car has parked for a decade often behaves completely differently from the rest.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look for Oil and Contamination</h3>
          <p>
            Dark shadows in the parking area are oil that has soaked into the slab, and no coating will bond over them. Surface degreasing helps, but oil that has been wicking down for years has to be pulled back out. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> covers poultices and microbial cleaners, which are the only methods that reach below the surface. Plan this work weeks ahead of coating day, because a poultice cycle takes days and stubborn stains need several.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Cracks and Spalling</h3>
          <p>
            Map every crack, pit and spalled edge now. Hairline shrinkage cracks can be filled with an epoxy crack filler and coated over. Cracks with vertical displacement, or ones that open and close seasonally, will telegraph through any coating and should be treated as control joints rather than defects to hide. Spalled areas need a patching mortar, not a thicker coat of epoxy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Confirm the Slab Is Old Enough</h3>
          <p>
            New concrete needs 28 days minimum, and the constraint is water, not strength. A slab reaches most of its design strength quickly, but the mixing water keeps working its way out for weeks. Coat too early and the vapor pushing up from underneath collects at the back of the film and lifts it into blisters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Moisture Testing, and Why It Matters More Than Anything</h2>
          <p>
            A garage slab poured directly on grade without a vapor barrier — which describes an enormous number of homes built before the 1990s — pumps ground moisture upward continuously. Epoxy is a vapor barrier. Put one on top of an active moisture drive and the water has nowhere to go but into the bond line, where it produces blisters, white deposits at the interface, and eventually sheet delamination.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Plastic Sheet Test</h3>
          <p>
            Tape an 18-inch square of clear plastic to the slab, sealing all four edges, and leave it for 24 hours. Condensation on the underside or a visibly darkened patch of concrete means moisture is moving through. Do this in at least three places, including the area closest to the driveway apron and the lowest corner of the garage. It is a screening test rather than a measurement, and a negative result is not conclusive on a slab you have reason to doubt.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Calcium Chloride and In-Slab Probes</h3>
          <p>
            The ASTM F1869 calcium chloride test measures how many pounds of moisture emit through 1,000 square feet in 24 hours. Most coatings want 3 pounds or less. The ASTM F2170 relative humidity probe drills into the slab and reads internal humidity directly, and most systems want 75 to 80 percent or below. Kits for either test cost less than the epoxy does, and running one is far cheaper than grinding a failed floor off.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">If the Slab Is Wet</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              You are not stuck, but you cannot ignore it. A moisture-mitigating epoxy primer — a 100 percent solids product designed to tolerate high vapor emission — is the standard answer, and it adds roughly $1 to $2 per square foot. The alternative is a breathable finish such as a penetrating densifier or a stain rather than a film-forming coating. What does not work is hoping, or adding a second coat of standard epoxy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="grind-vs-etch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Grinding vs Acid Etching</h2>
          <p>
            This is the decision that separates coatings that last a decade from coatings that peel in a year.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[560px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Diamond Grinding</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Acid Etching</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Removes sealer and curing compound</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Removes laitance</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Partially</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical profile</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">CSP 2-3</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">CSP 1-2</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mess</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dust, controllable with a vac</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acidic rinse water to manage</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost for a two-car garage</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$200-400 DIY</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-80</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Suitable for two-part epoxy</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Always</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only on bare porous slabs</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Acid etching has a legitimate but narrow use case: a bare, uncoated, genuinely porous slab that will receive a thin one-part garage floor paint. Muriatic acid or a safer citric or phosphoric etch opens the surface and gives that kind of product enough to hold. It is cheap, it needs no rented equipment, and it is why every hardware store kit includes a packet.
          </p>
          <p>
            What it cannot do is remove anything. Acid reacts with cement paste at the open surface; it does not touch a sealer film sitting on top, and it cannot dissolve a curing compound. Etch a sealed slab and you have accomplished nothing except spending an afternoon and creating acidic waste water. Etching also demands complete neutralization and rinsing, because residual acid left in the pores attacks the coating from beneath.
          </p>
          <p>
            Grinding does everything etching does and more, in less time, with dust that a vacuum handles. If you are renting a machine for a day anyway, the marginal cost over an etch kit is small relative to the cost of the coating itself — and enormously small relative to redoing the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grinding" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How to Grind a Garage Floor</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose the Machine</h3>
          <p>
            For a two-car garage, a walk-behind planetary grinder in the 10 to 20 inch range is the right tool and rents for around $100 to $250 a day. A hand-held 7-inch grinder with a cup wheel and a dust shroud handles edges and corners, and it is exhausting as a primary tool for anything larger than a small shed. Rent the dust extractor with the machine; a standard shop vac clogs in minutes and the silica dust is a genuine health hazard.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose the Diamonds</h3>
          <p>
            Use metal-bond segments in the 30 to 40 grit range for coating prep. Softer bonds cut hard concrete and harder bonds cut soft concrete, which feels backwards until you understand that the bond has to erode to expose fresh diamond. Resin-bond diamonds and anything finer than about 80 grit are polishing tools and produce a surface too closed for epoxy to grip.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Work the Floor in Two Directions</h3>
          <p>
            Grind the whole floor in overlapping passes in one direction, then repeat perpendicular to the first pass. The cross pattern is what evens out the profile and catches the shiny strips that a single direction leaves between passes. Keep the machine moving at a steady walking pace — dwelling in one spot digs a low place you will see through the finished floor.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Get the Edges</h3>
          <p>
            A walk-behind grinder cannot reach the last inch or two along the walls, and that perimeter strip is where coatings most often fail visibly. Use the hand grinder with a cup wheel around the entire perimeter, around any posts, and into the corners. This is slow, unpleasant work and it is the difference between a professional-looking edge and a visible untreated band.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Judge the Result by Feel</h3>
          <p>
            A correctly prepared slab feels like 60 to 80 grit sandpaper under a bare palm, looks uniformly matte with no reflective patches, and takes a drop of water instantly across the whole surface. That texture is the CSP 2 to 3 profile the coating manufacturers are asking for, and it is the practical test that matters more than any specification number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repairs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Cracks, Pits and Oil — Repair Order</h2>
          <p>
            Repairs go after grinding, not before. Grinding first exposes the true extent of the damage and gives the patch material clean concrete to bond to, and it also means the grinder does not tear out fresh filler.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Static Cracks</h3>
          <p>
            Chase hairline cracks open slightly with a crack chaser wheel or a grinder blade, vacuum the dust out, and fill with a low-viscosity epoxy or polyurea crack filler. Overfill slightly and shave flush once cured. Filling without chasing gives the filler almost no surface to hold, and the crack reappears through the coating within a season.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Spalls and Pits</h3>
          <p>
            Use a polymer-modified patching mortar or an epoxy mortar made by mixing your coating resin with clean silica sand. Feather the edges so the repair does not create a visible bump, and grind the patch flush once it has cured. Deep pits filled with straight epoxy tend to sink and to cure hot, so sand-filled repairs are better for anything more than a shallow divot.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Control Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Decide deliberately. Filling saw-cut control joints with a flexible polyurea joint filler gives a seamless look and works well where the slab is stable. Leaving them open and coating into them preserves the joint's ability to move and prevents a random crack forming elsewhere. What is not a good idea is filling them with rigid epoxy on a slab that still moves seasonally.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Contamination</h3>
          <p>
            After grinding, re-check the old parking spots by sprinkling water. If a patch still refuses to absorb, oil is present below the surface and needs to come out before coating — degrease, poultice, or in stubborn cases grind deeper. Coating over contaminated concrete produces a fisheye pattern in the wet film, and once you see that the only fix is to keep going and hope, which rarely ends well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="final-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Final Pre-Coating Checklist</h2>
          <p>
            Run through this before you open a single can. Every item is something that cannot be corrected once the epoxy is down.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 m-0">
              <li>Slab is at least 28 days old, and moisture tested if there is any doubt.</li>
              <li>Water sprinkled anywhere on the floor absorbs within a minute.</li>
              <li>Surface feels like medium-grit sandpaper with no glossy patches.</li>
              <li>Every crack and pit is filled, cured and ground flush.</li>
              <li>Perimeter and corners are ground, not just the open field.</li>
              <li>Floor is vacuumed twice, including the wall joint and door track.</li>
              <li>Slab temperature is within the product range and above the dew point.</li>
              <li>Products, rollers, mixing tools and enough helpers are staged and ready.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p>
            One practical note on scheduling: the vacuum pass should happen immediately before coating, not the night before. Garage dust settles overnight, and dust in the film is the most common cosmetic complaint on an otherwise sound floor. With the prep finished, the application itself is straightforward — our walkthrough on <a href="/how-to-epoxy-garage-floor/">how to epoxy a garage floor</a> takes it from the primer coat onward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you have to grind concrete before epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grinding is the professional standard and the only prep that reliably removes old sealer, curing compound and laitance while opening the surface. Acid etching can work on bare, porous, uncoated concrete, but it does nothing to a sealed slab and leaves a shallower profile. If the floor has ever been sealed or coated, grinding is not optional.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if concrete is sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sprinkle water in several places across the slab. If it soaks in and darkens the concrete within a minute, the surface is open and porous. If it beads or sits on top, there is a sealer or curing compound present and it must be ground off before any coating goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What grit should you use to grind concrete for epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30 to 40 grit metal-bond diamond is the usual choice for coating prep, producing roughly a CSP 2 to CSP 3 profile. Finer resin-bond diamonds polish the surface, which is the opposite of what a coating needs. For a thick self-leveling system, a shot blaster or a coarser profile is better.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does concrete need to cure before applying epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                New concrete needs a minimum of 28 days, and the reason is moisture rather than strength. A slab that still holds construction water will drive vapor into the back of the coating and cause blisters or delamination. Test with a calcium chloride or relative humidity probe before coating anything younger than a few months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is acid etching good enough for epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is adequate for thin one-part garage floor paints on bare, porous concrete and marginal for a real two-part epoxy. Etching only reaches an open surface, must be neutralized and rinsed completely, and leaves a profile roughly equivalent to 120 grit sandpaper. Most kit failures we see trace to etching a slab that should have been ground.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you test concrete for moisture before epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The quick screen is a plastic sheet test: tape an 18-inch square of plastic to the slab on all four sides and check it after 24 hours. Condensation underneath or a darkened patch of concrete means moisture is moving. For a definitive number, use an ASTM F1869 calcium chloride kit or an ASTM F2170 in-slab humidity probe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to prep a garage floor for epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A weekend grinder rental runs $100 to $250 plus $50 to $150 for diamond segments and a dust shroud, so a DIY prep on a two-car garage lands around $200 to $400. Professional surface preparation is typically quoted at $1 to $3 per square foot depending on what has to come off.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Prep Mistakes That Guarantee Failure</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Etching a Sealed Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acid cannot reach through a sealer film. The floor looks etched because it is wet, dries unchanged, and the coating peels within the year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Moisture Testing on an Old Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pre-1990s garages frequently have no vapor barrier. A test kit costs less than one gallon of epoxy and prevents the single most expensive failure mode.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Perimeter Untouched</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The band a walk-behind cannot reach is still sealed concrete. Coating over it leaves a visible frame that lifts first and spreads inward.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Over Oil Shadows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fisheyes appear in the wet film within minutes and cannot be rolled out. Pull the oil first with a poultice or grind deeper into clean concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rushing a New Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twenty-eight days is a minimum, not a target. Blisters from trapped construction water are not repairable without removing the coating.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Concrete Preparation Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy almost never fails because of the resin. It fails because the slab underneath was not opened, dried, or cleaned properly first.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acid Etching a Hard-Troweled Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Etching is the instruction in most kits because it is cheap, and on a dense, power-troweled garage slab it barely opens the surface. The floor looks prepared and delaminates a year later. Diamond grinding is the reliable method, and a day's rental is the best money in the whole project.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapour moving up through the slab pushes epoxy off from behind, and no amount of surface preparation prevents it. Tape a two-foot square of plastic down for 24 to 48 hours. Condensation on the underside means you need a moisture-mitigating primer, not a better grinder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Oil Contamination in Place</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy bonds to concrete and not to oil, so a stain that looks faint on the surface becomes a fisheye or a bare patch in the finish. Degrease thoroughly, and treat deep-set stains with a poultice. Where the contamination will not come out, grind that area down to clean concrete.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating Before the Slab Is Dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Grinding and washing both leave the slab wet well below the surface, and epoxy applied over it will not bond. Allow the concrete to dry fully — often a day or more after wet grinding — and confirm with a plastic-sheet test rather than by touch.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

