        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Preparing concrete for epoxy</strong> comes down to three questions: is the slab dry, is it clean down to sound concrete, and does it have enough texture for the resin to key into. Grinding answers the second and third in one afternoon; a moisture test answers the first for the price of a kit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Etching has a place on bare, porous slabs receiving thin coatings, and almost nowhere else. If the floor has ever been sealed, if a builder sprayed a curing compound on it, or if you are installing a real two-part system, <strong class="text-white">rent the grinder</strong>. It is the cheapest insurance in the entire project.
          </p>
